@endnode

@node g-what-you-need "What you need"
@next g-the-first-program

@{b}What you need@{ub}

  - AmigaOS 2.04 or newer, any 680x0.
  - LIBS:json.library: the build for the processor (build/000, 020 or 060,
    see the README). There is no FPU build, because the library does no
    floating-point maths.
  - The headers in include/: libraries/json.h (types and constants),
    proto/json.h, inline/json.h (the calls, for gcc) and
    clib/json_protos.h (the prototypes). json.sfd describes the calls and
    their registers, for making headers for other languages (sfdc or
    fd2pragma).
  - Your program's stack: the library's deepest call uses under 1 KB.

@endnode

@node g-the-first-program "The first program"
@next g-how-it-works
@prev g-what-you-need

@{b}The first program@{ub}

Reading the weather from a JSON text:

    #include <stdio.h>
    #include <proto/exec.h>
    #include <proto/json.h>

    struct Library *JsonBase;

    int main(void)
    {
        static const char text[] =
            "{ \\"city\\": \\"G\\xc3\\xb6teborg\\", \\"temp\\": 8.25, \\"wind\\": [\\"SW\\", 7] }";
        struct JsonError err;
        JsonDoc *doc;
        JsonNode *root;
        char city[40];

        if (!(JsonBase = OpenLibrary(JSONNAME, 0))) {
            printf("needs json.library\\n");
            return 20;
        }
        if (!(doc = JsonParse(text, -1, 0, &err))) {
            printf("line %ld col %ld: %s\\n", (long)err.Line, (long)err.Col, err.Message);
            CloseLibrary(JsonBase);
            return 10;
        }
        root = JsonRoot(doc);
        JsonLatin1(JsonGet(root, "city"), city, sizeof(city));          /* Amiga text */
        printf("%s: %ld.%02ld degrees, wind %ld m/s\\n", city,
               (long)JsonScaled(JsonGet(root, "temp"), 2, 0) / 100,       /* 825 */
               (long)JsonScaled(JsonGet(root, "temp"), 2, 0) % 100,
               (long)JsonInt(JsonFind(root, "wind[1]"), 0));             /* 7 */
        JsonFree(doc);                                                   /* every node with it */
        CloseLibrary(JsonBase);
        return 0;
    }

It prints Göteborg: 8.25 degrees, wind 7 m/s. Build with bebbo's gcc:
m68k-amigaos-gcc -Os -noixemul -Wno-pointer-sign -I<json>/include
weather.c -o weather.

@endnode

@node g-how-it-works "How it works"
@next g-reading
@prev g-the-first-program

@{b}How it works@{ub}

@{b}A document holds a tree of nodes.@{ub} JsonParse (from text) or JsonLoad (from
a file) returns a JsonDoc *. Inside it, every value is a JsonNode *: an
object, an array, a string, a number, true/false (a bool) or null. Objects
and arrays have members; an object's members have names. JsonRoot(doc) is
the top value.

@{b}The document owns everything.@{ub} Nodes are never freed one by one:
JsonFree(doc) frees the whole tree at once. A node pointer is valid until
its document is freed. Strings the library hands out (JsonString,
JsonName, JsonNumText) live in the document too.

@{b}NULL is always safe.@{ub} Every call accepts NULL for a node and answers
"nothing" (JT_NONE, NULL, 0 or the default you give). So a chain like
JsonInt(JsonGet(JsonGet(root, "a"), "b"), -1) needs no checks in between:
if anything is missing, the answer is -1.

@{b}Small machines first.@{ub} The library never calls itself recursively, so a
deeply nested file cannot overflow the stack. It does no floating-point
maths and no 32-bit multiply or divide in its loops (a 68000 has neither),
and it gets memory in a few big blocks per document, not per node.

@endnode

@node g-reading "Reading"
@next g-paths
@prev g-how-it-works

@{b}Reading@{ub}

    JsonDoc *JsonParse(text, len, flags, &err);     /* len -1: up to the 0 */
    JsonDoc *JsonLoad("PROGDIR:settings.json", flags, &err);

Both return NULL on failure and fill in struct JsonError (pass NULL if you
do not need it):

  Message
    what was wrong, in English: "expected , or }", "a string is not
      closed", "text that is not UTF-8"...
  Line, Col
    where, from 1 (Col counts bytes)
  Pos
    the byte, from 0

Without flags the text must be strict JSON (RFC 8259). The library passes
all 283 must-accept and must-refuse cases of JSONTestSuite (https://github.com/nst/JSONTestSuite).

@{b}Walking@{ub} a tree:

  JsonType(n)
    JT_OBJECT, JT_ARRAY, JT_STRING, JT_NUMBER, JT_BOOL, JT_NULL, or
      JT_NONE for NULL
  JsonGet(object, "name")
    a member by name (if a name comes twice, the last one, as JavaScript
      does)
  JsonAt(array, i)
    the i-th item, from 0; negative counts from the end (-1 is the last)
  JsonFind(node, "a.b[2].c")
    a node by path (below)
  JsonCount(n)
    how many members an object or array has
  JsonFirst(n), JsonNext(n)
    the members one by one
  JsonParent(n)
    the object or array a node is in
  JsonName(n)
    an object member's name

Every member of an object, in order:

    for (m = JsonFirst(obj); m; m = JsonNext(m))
        printf("%s is of type %ld\\n", JsonName(m), (long)JsonType(m));

JsonFirst/JsonNext is the fast way through a big array: JsonAt(array, i)
walks from the start each time.

@endnode

@node g-paths "Paths"
@next g-values
@prev g-reading

@{b}Paths@{ub}

JsonFind(node, path) goes several steps at once:

  weather.city
    the member city of the member weather
  days[0]
    the first item of the array days
  days[-1].temp
    temp in the last item of days
  ["a.b"]
    a member whose name has dots or brackets in it (\\" and \\\\ inside)
  (empty)
    the node itself

A path that does not lead anywhere gives NULL, never a crash.

@endnode

@node g-values "Values"
@next g-numbers-without-floating-point
@prev g-paths

@{b}Values@{ub}

  JsonString(n)
    For: a string
    Gives: the text, UTF-8, 0-terminated; NULL for anything else
  JsonLength(n)
    For: a string or number
    Gives: its length in bytes (a string may hold a 0 byte, written
      \\u0000)
  JsonLatin1(n, buf, size)
    For: a string or number
    Gives: the text as Amiga text (below)
  JsonInt(n, def)
    For: a number
    Gives: the nearest whole number (8.5 is 9, -8.5 is -9), kept within a
      LONG
  JsonScaled(n, places, def)
    For: a number
    Gives: a fixed-point number (below)
  JsonNumText(n)
    For: a number
    Gives: its text exactly as written: "8.25", "1e-3"
  JsonBool(n, def)
    For: true or false
    Gives: 1 or 0

JsonInt and JsonScaled also take @{b}a string that is exactly a number@{ub}: some
web services send "temp": "8.5". Anything else gives def, the default you
pass, so you can tell "missing" from 0:

    LONG port = JsonInt(JsonGet(cfg, "port"), 8080);     /* 8080 when it is not set */

@endnode

@node g-numbers-without-floating-point "Numbers without floating point"
@next g-text-utf-8-and-latin-1
@prev g-values

@{b}Numbers without floating point@{ub}

A plain 68000 has no FPU, so the library keeps numbers as their text and
hands them out as whole numbers. For decimals, JsonScaled(n, places, def)
gives the number times 10^places, rounded:

  @{b}Number@{ub}  @{b}JsonScaled(n, 0, -)@{ub}  @{b}(n, 1, -)@{ub}  @{b}(n, 2, -)@{ub}
  ------  -------------------  ---------  ---------
  8.25    8                    83         825
  -0.5    -1                   -5         -50
  1e3     1000                 10000      100000

That is how money, temperatures and percentages are usually handled on
small machines anyway: cents, tenths of a degree. Values too big for a
LONG stop at its largest or smallest (2147483647 / -2147483648). places is
0 to 9.

A program that does want floating point takes JsonNumText(n) and converts
it with its own maths library (atof, mathieeedoubbas.library).

@endnode

@node g-text-utf-8-and-latin-1 "Text: UTF-8 and Latin-1"
@next g-building-and-writing
@prev g-numbers-without-floating-point

@{b}Text: UTF-8 and Latin-1@{ub}

JSON text is always UTF-8 (Unicode), and so is everything inside the
library. Amiga programs mostly use Latin-1. The library converts at the
edges:

  - @{b}Reading:@{ub} JsonLatin1(n, buf, size) copies a string as Latin-1 into buf,
    ended with a 0. Characters that Latin-1 does not have become the
    nearest it has: L as L, EUR as E, curly quotes as ", dashes as -,
    anything else as ?. It returns the full length, even if buf was too
    small, so JsonLatin1(n, NULL, 0) asks how big a buffer you need.
  - @{b}Building:@{ub} JsonNewString(doc, text, len, JSF_LATIN1) takes Amiga text.
    Without the flag the text is taken as UTF-8; any byte that is not
    valid UTF-8 is still taken as Latin-1, so the library never writes
    broken JSON.
  - @{b}Writing:@{ub} JWF_ASCII writes every character beyond ASCII as ö: pure
    ASCII that any program and any 7-bit channel accepts.

@endnode

@node g-building-and-writing "Building and writing"
@next g-settings-files-relaxed-reading
@prev g-text-utf-8-and-latin-1

@{b}Building and writing@{ub}

    JsonDoc *doc = @{"JsonNewDoc()" link "JsonNewDoc"};
    JsonNode *o = JsonNewObject(doc), *days = JsonNewArray(doc);

    JsonSetRoot(doc, o);
    JsonAdd(o, "city", JsonNewString(doc, "G\\xf6teborg", -1, JSF_LATIN1));
    JsonAdd(o, "temp", JsonNewNumber(doc, "8.25"));
    JsonAdd(o, "sunny", JsonNewBool(doc, TRUE));
    JsonAdd(o, "days", days);
    JsonAdd(days, NULL, JsonNewInt(doc, 1));            /* arrays: no name */

    len = JsonWrite(o, buf, sizeof(buf), 0);            /* {"city":"Göteborg","temp":8.25,...} */
    JsonWriteFH(o, fh, JWF_PRETTY);                     /* to a file, two spaces an indent */
    JsonFree(doc);

  - JsonNew... make a node in the document, not yet anywhere in the tree.
    JsonAdd puts it into an object (with a name) or an array (name NULL).
    Adding a name that is already there replaces the old member.
  - A failed JsonNew... returns NULL and JsonAdd refuses NULL, so the
    nested calls above need no checks: if memory ran out, that member is
    just missing.
  - JsonAdd refuses a node that is already in the tree, one from another
    document, and a node added into itself.
  - JsonRemove(n) takes a node out (it can be added again elsewhere);
    JsonSetRoot sets the top.
  - JsonNewNumber(doc, "3.14") takes the number's text and checks it is a
    JSON number; JsonNewInt(doc, n) takes a LONG.
  - JsonWrite works like snprintf: it returns the full length, writes at
    most size - 1 bytes and a 0. JsonWrite(n, NULL, 0, flags) measures.
    JsonWriteFH writes to an open file and ends with a newline; it returns
    FALSE if writing failed.
  - Any node can be written, not just the root: JsonWrite(JsonGet(root,
    "days"), ...).

@endnode

@node g-settings-files-relaxed-reading "Settings files: relaxed reading"
@next g-arexx
@prev g-building-and-writing

@{b}Settings files: relaxed reading@{ub}

People who write settings by hand leave comments and trailing commas, and
old files are in Latin-1. JPF_RELAXED accepts all three:

    doc = JsonLoad("ENVARC:MyProgram.json", JPF_RELAXED, &err);

    // MyProgram settings
    {
        "colours": ["black", "white",],    /* a comma at the end is fine */
        "name": "Åsa"                      (in Latin-1: fine too)
    }

The flags one by one: JPF_COMMENTS (// and /* */), JPF_TRAILING (a comma
before ] or }), JPF_LATIN1 (bytes that are not UTF-8 are Latin-1). Writing
always produces strict JSON.

@endnode

@node g-arexx "ARexx"
@next g-versions
@prev g-settings-files-relaxed-reading

@{b}ARexx@{ub}

json.library is also an ARexx function library:

    /* the weather */
    CALL ADDLIB('json.library', 0, -30, 0)
    j = JSONLOAD('RAM:weather.json')
    IF j = '' THEN DO
      SAY 'cannot read it:' JSONERROR()
      EXIT 10
    END
    SAY JSONGET(j, 'city') 'has' JSONGET(j, 'temp') 'degrees'
    DO i = 0 TO JSONCOUNT(j, 'days') - 1
      SAY 'day' i':' JSONGET(j, 'days['i'].temp')
    END
    CALL JSONFREE(j)

An ARexx script starts with a comment, and ADDLIB tells ARexx about the
library once (it stays added until the next reboot; adding it again does
no harm). Documents are @{b}handles@{ub}, text like JSON1. Paths are the same as in
C. Text going in and coming out is Latin-1; the JSON text of JSONTEXT and
JSONSAVE is UTF-8 (or ASCII with the ASCII option).

  JSONPARSE(text, [options])
    reads JSON text: a handle, or '' (then JSONERROR() says why). Options:
      RELAXED
  JSONLOAD(file, [options])
    reads a file: a handle or ''. Options: RELAXED
  JSONNEW([ARRAY])
    a new, empty document (an object, or an array): a handle
  JSONFREE(handle)
    frees it
  JSONGET(handle, path, [default])
    a value as text: strings as they are, numbers as written, true/false
      as 1/0, null as '', objects and arrays as JSON. Missing: the default
      or ''
  JSONTYPE(handle, path)
    OBJECT, ARRAY, STRING, NUMBER, BOOL, NULL, or '' if missing
  JSONCOUNT(handle, path)
    how many members (0 if not an object or array)
  JSONNAME(handle, path, n)
    the name of an object's n-th member, from 0 ('' past the end)
  JSONSET(handle, path, value, [type])
    sets a value, making the objects and arrays on the way; [n] one past
      the end adds to an array. Type: STRING, NUMBER, BOOL (1/0,
      TRUE/FALSE, YES/NO, ON/OFF), NULL, OBJECT, ARRAY, JSON (the value is
      JSON text). Without a type, a value that is a JSON number becomes a
      number, anything else text (01234 stays text). 1, or 0 when it
      cannot be done (JSONERROR() says why)
  JSONREMOVE(handle, path)
    removes a value: 1, or 0 if it was not there
  JSONTEXT(handle, [path], [options])
    the document (or the value at path) as JSON text. Options: PRETTY,
      ASCII
  JSONSAVE(handle, file, [options])
    writes the document to a file: 1 or 0. Options: PRETTY, ASCII
  JSONERROR()
    why the last JSONPARSE, JSONLOAD, JSONSET, JSONSAVE, or a too-long
      JSONGET/JSONTEXT failed, like 3:7 expected : after a name
      (line:column)

Building in ARexx:

    /* building */
    CALL ADDLIB('json.library', 0, -30, 0)
    k = JSONNEW()
    CALL JSONSET(k, 'city', 'Göteborg')
    CALL JSONSET(k, 'temp', 8.5)
    CALL JSONSET(k, 'days[0].temp', 7)
    CALL JSONSET(k, 'days[1].temp', 9)
    SAY JSONTEXT(k, '', 'ASCII')
    /* {"city":"G\\u00f6teborg","temp":8.5,"days":[{"temp":7},{"temp":9}]} */
    CALL JSONSAVE(k, 'RAM:out.json', 'PRETTY')
    CALL JSONFREE(k)

  - @{b}Free your handles.@{ub} They are shared by all scripts (a handle can be
    passed to another script) and live until JSONFREE, or until the
    library leaves memory.
  - @{b}ARexx strings are at most 65535 bytes.@{ub} A bigger JSONTEXT or JSONGET
    gives '' and JSONERROR() says so; save big documents with JSONSAVE.
    Files of any size can be loaded.
  - A wrong handle or a missing argument is ARexx error 17 or 18, which
    stops the script; things that can simply fail (bad JSON, a path that
    cannot be set, a file that cannot be opened) give '' or 0 and leave
    the reason in JSONERROR().

@endnode

@node g-versions "Versions"
@next g-rules-and-traps
@prev g-arexx

@{b}Versions@{ub}

While json.library is young its version is @{b}0@{ub} and its @{b}revision@{ub} counts up
(0.1, 0.2...). Open version 0 and check the revision before using newer
calls:

  @{b}Revision@{ub}  @{b}Added@{ub}
  --------  ----------
  0.1       everything

From 1.0 on the version will count and OpenLibrary(JSONNAME, n) will do
the check. Calls are never removed or moved, so a program built for an
older revision keeps working with every newer one.

@endnode

@node g-rules-and-traps "Rules and traps"
@prev g-versions

@{b}Rules and traps@{ub}

  - @{b}Free documents with@{ub} JsonFree@{b}.@{ub} Not nodes, not strings: the document
    owns them.
  - @{b}Never use a node after its document is freed@{ub}, and never mix nodes of
    two documents: JsonAdd refuses them.
  - @{b}Strings from the library are read-only@{ub} and UTF-8. Copy with JsonLatin1
    for Amiga text.
  - JsonString @{b}stops at a 0 byte@{ub} inside a string (\\u0000 in the JSON);
    JsonLength tells the real length.
  - @{b}Big arrays:@{ub} walk them with JsonFirst/JsonNext; JsonAt(a, i) in a loop
    goes from the start every time.
  - @{b}Documents are not shared between tasks@{ub} without your own locking.
    Different documents in different tasks are fine: the library keeps
    nothing per program.
  - @{b}Duplicate names:@{ub} JsonGet gives the last one; JsonFirst/JsonNext see
    them all.

@endnode

